    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "def3c83d_c180b088",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 42,
      "author": {
        "id": 1201522
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-04T08:40:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "For me this is the overall policy which makes bL (dual-gear) and tri-gear specific incarnations of n-gear:\n\n(1) Use an available CPU in the requested (by util \u0026 u_clamp) \n    gear. (meet (QoS) request)\n(2) If there is no CPU in the requested gear try to go up one gear\n    step by step. (Over-provisioning (meet (QoS) request))\n(3) If there is no CPU in the upper gears, use the highest   \n    available gear. (Under-provisioning (Best Effort))\n(4) Use given fallback CPU (Emergency exit (Best Effort))",
      "revId": "841d40ab0efc43c9ee06d99db096bc6e3d8987d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "66e89c23_66213792",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 42,
      "author": {
        "id": 1569742
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-04T09:41:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yep, sounds like a good description of what this tries to achieve :)\n\nAn alternative solution would be, select an idle CPU candidate in each pd where the task fits and use the EM to decide which is best. That\u0027d be more generic that trying to find the smallest possible idle CPU, but we need profiling on real HW to make sure we could afford the overhead of the energy diff.",
      "parentUuid": "def3c83d_c180b088",
      "revId": "841d40ab0efc43c9ee06d99db096bc6e3d8987d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "145f7f26_c48d7920",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 42,
      "author": {
        "id": 1201522
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-04T09:55:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "With PD \u003d\u003d Gear.\n\nThat would be smarter than required.\n\nA system integrator says task T has u_clamp UC_t.\n\nWhat positive outcome would you expect from this?\n\nThe EM knows that it can run T (with UC_t) more efficient on PD_x+1 (freq_x+1_y) than on PD_x (freq_x_i)?\n\nThis wouldn\u0027t work on today\u0027s tri-gear in which Big is Medium with a higher freq range but could work in general.\n\nWe should try to formalize a rule here which is broad enough for all cases but still reflects reality.\n\nIf we can achieve to map this rule into code then I would say tri-gear mission accomplished.",
      "parentUuid": "66e89c23_66213792",
      "revId": "841d40ab0efc43c9ee06d99db096bc6e3d8987d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2e6434e1_f55eab8c",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 42,
      "author": {
        "id": 1569742
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-10-04T10:11:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e With PD \u003d\u003d Gear.\n\nRight.\n\n\u003e That would be smarter than required.\n\u003e \n\u003e A system integrator says task T has u_clamp UC_t.\n\u003e \n\u003e What positive outcome would you expect from this?\n\u003e \n\u003e The EM knows that it can run T (with UC_t) more efficient on PD_x+1 (freq_x+1_y) than on PD_x (freq_x_i)?\n\nYep, for example. This can be true for a number of reasons. For instance, say you already have a bunch of tasks running on PD_x, if placing T there involves to raise the frequency of the whole PD, you might prefer to place the task on PD_x+1 if it has no tasks. Depending on the EM, of course.\n\n\u003e This wouldn\u0027t work on today\u0027s tri-gear in which Big is Medium with a higher freq range but could work in general.\n\nWhy would it not work on today\u0027s tri gear ? We know that going for the smaller CPUs isn\u0027t always the best choice energy-wise. That\u0027s actually why we have an EM to being with :)\n\nThe main issue I\u0027d see with this approach is the potential scheduling overhead, so this definitely wants more work/testing before we can seriously consider it.",
      "parentUuid": "145f7f26_c48d7920",
      "revId": "841d40ab0efc43c9ee06d99db096bc6e3d8987d2",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
